  "query_preview": "..A strong high pressure system will remain centered\nover or near Arizona through Friday and continue daytime \ntemperatures in the mid 80 to lower 90s in the mountains to 105 to\n110 at elevations below 4000 feet in the forecast area. Due to \nthe hot temperatures at lower elevations an Extreme Heat Warning \nis in effect for the lower portions of the Grand Canyon as well as\nYavapai county through Friday. Enough moisture will remain for \nthe daily development of isolated to scattered showers and\nthunderstorms during the afternoon and evening hours, mainly at \nhigher elevations. The risk for flooding or hail is expected to \nremain limited, but gusts up to 40 mph will be possible in or \nnear thunderstorms.\n\nStarting Saturday, the high pressure center will shift eastward \nin response to a longwave trough and embedded disturbances moving \nover the western states. The trough will remain just far enough \nnorth and west to allow monsoon moisture to stream northward \nacross Arizona this weekend. Look for the daily influence of \ndaytime heating on thunderstorm development but with the addition \nof passing shortwave disturbances interacting with this moisture \n(classic transition season stuff), leading to an increase in\nshower and thunderstorm coverage, potentially becoming scattered\nto numerous. Uncertainty is still relatively high, but the \npattern could contain a decent amount of vertical wind shear which\ncould lead to more organized thunderstorms with an elevated \npotential to become severe. In addition, about 10-20% percent of \nmodel ensemble members are pointing toward a deeper moisture tap. \nIf the deeper moisture tap develops cooler trough related \ntemperatures aloft, organized vertical wind shear and passing \nenhancements to upward motion by the weak disturbances could lead \nto a quite active weekend, with potential risks including flash \nflooding, hail, and potentially severe wind gusts. Around Tuesday \nor Wednesday, the longwave trough is expected to push further \neastward and closer to Arizona, with long range ensembles showing \na potential drying trend as drier air from the west is pushed \ntowards Arizona.",
